#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6e6ab0-e1a2-5036-a674-a8a7f4eb423c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365929} {:answer/id #uuid "57b6c77a-6893-516a-8f91-0dedf6e9058a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365930} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e4895c-f6d4-5497-8a22-e8d6e6d2cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365931} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdb14d7-b5e7-5001-bea2-034d9be5ca5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e4895c-f6d4-5497-8a22-e8d6e6d2cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365931} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77f080d-827b-51df-b338-49ffb15b987a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94df4605-16e7-5b93-975e-109a28027292" :question/math-academy-id "q-343447" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\pi \\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{2}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{2}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4aa6e47-9d1e-5779-9908-ea76c7a10b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4aa6e47-9d1e-5779-9908-ea76c7a10b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365935} :answer-field/id #uuid "47512ec8-98e6-55de-bf8d-1850316def72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a214757-6d04-5715-a737-0db47732a8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-343727" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+3y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+3y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-3x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-1.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ -1 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=-{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-{e}^{-3x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91505a79-2ea5-54d7-95d8-90e5cea46420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91505a79-2ea5-54d7-95d8-90e5cea46420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365938} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3102c8-b53d-52e0-8f47-cacae0ffde3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bafe4c7-5c26-53ec-8fd3-af5a2bac498d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343732" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=f(x),\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=5.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{4(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 5 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=5{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼5{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab86f29f-86b9-5139-b7b9-52289787dff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365962} {:answer/id #uuid "13c96fa5-5aad-5a39-9717-f46276309fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365963} {:answer/id #uuid "99149897-b6f0-53e2-8cbd-173ffe25f56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365964} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d6966-abab-5d76-b3f7-3f492dcb3ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99149897-b6f0-53e2-8cbd-173ffe25f56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365964} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e8b6be-52c3-5fb8-87bd-b14fedfa5492" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c618b807-0d3f-5c96-b840-87c2666fd829" :question/math-academy-id "q-344047" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm 2\\pi \\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B(2\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B(2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B(2\\pi ) & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{2n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"}]}
